WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.847
Look around you.

2
00:00:02.147 --> 00:00:04.326
Everything feels real.

3
00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:07.330
But what if none of it is…

4
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.507
and you're just a brain,

5
00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:10.764
in a vat?

6
00:00:12.464 --> 00:00:13.852
Brain in a Vat.

7
00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:25.242
In 1641, René Descartes sat by his fire and asked what, if anything, he could know for certain.

8
00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.547
He imagined an evil demon,

9
00:00:28.547 --> 00:00:33.514
as powerful as it was cunning, devoting all its energy to deceiving him.

10
00:00:33.914 --> 00:00:38.680
Every sight, every sound, even his own body, might be an illusion.

11
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.237
In 1981, Hilary Putnam brought the demon into the laboratory.

12
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:51.362
Imagine that an evil scientist has removed your brain,

13
00:00:51.562 --> 00:00:55.503
and placed it in a vat of nutrients that keeps it alive.

14
00:00:56.203 --> 00:01:00.535
Its nerve endings are wired to a super-scientific computer,

15
00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:04.688
which feeds it exactly the signals a real body would.

16
00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:09.208
You would see, hear and feel a perfectly normal world.

17
00:01:09.608 --> 00:01:13.257
Everything would seem exactly as it does right now.

18
00:01:13.957 --> 00:01:18.864
So how could you ever know that you're not a brain in a vat?

19
00:01:26.864 --> 00:01:29.407
Here's why it matters.

20
00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:33.987
If you know you have hands, you know you're not a brain in a vat.

21
00:01:34.487 --> 00:01:38.892
But nothing you could ever experience would tell you that you're not.

22
00:01:39.392 --> 00:01:42.768
So, it seems, you don't know you have hands.

23
00:01:43.368 --> 00:01:47.028
And the same goes for almost everything you believe.

24
00:01:50.028 --> 00:01:53.163
Putnam's answer was surprising.

25
00:01:53.563 --> 00:01:58.814
Words, he argued, can only refer to things we are causally connected to.

26
00:01:59.214 --> 00:02:06.178
A brain that has always lived in a vat has never met a real vat, only computer images of one.

27
00:02:06.578 --> 00:02:13.114
So when it thinks, “I am a brain in a vat,” it is talking about image-vats, and the thought is false.

28
00:02:13.614 --> 00:02:17.914
So, Putnam concluded, we cannot be brains in vats.

29
00:02:20.914 --> 00:02:23.642
Not everyone was convinced.

30
00:02:24.042 --> 00:02:32.402
The argument says nothing about a brain that was put in a vat only last night. Its words would still mean real vats.

31
00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:42.139
And Thomas Nagel raised a stranger worry: perhaps we can't even think the truth of what we are, because we lack the concepts to do it.

32
00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:48.181
So, could you ever know? And how?

33
00:02:49.381 --> 00:02:56.122
This is the brain in a vat in its simplest form. Philosophers have since built many variations,

34
00:02:56.122 --> 00:03:01.561
like the idea that our entire universe might be a computer simulation.

35
00:03:02.161 --> 00:03:05.623
The scenario is deliberately extreme.

36
00:03:05.923 --> 00:03:12.294
Push doubt as far as it will go, and it shows us what we really mean when we say we know something.
